Ryujinx shader translator: also log a barrier on a potentially divergent block when it is kept (GitHub issue #45).

Upstream logs such a barrier only when SupportsShaderBarrierDivergence is false, which its Vulkan backend answers on
Intel ("Vendor != Vendor.Intel"), and then removes it. The harness answers true unless --no-divergent-barriers is
given, so without this the summary could not say whether the game has any. The emitted SPIR-V does not change.

--- a/CodeGen/Spirv/Instructions.cs	2026-11-18 10:21:07
+++ b/CodeGen/Spirv/Instructions.cs	2026-11-18 10:21:07
@@ -241,14 +241,21 @@
         {
             // Barrier on divergent control flow paths may cause the GPU to hang,
             // so skip emitting the barrier for those cases.
-            if (!context.HostCapabilities.SupportsShaderBarrierDivergence &&
-                (context.CurrentBlock.Type != AstBlockType.Main || context.MayHaveReturned || !context.IsMainFunction))
+            bool divergent = context.CurrentBlock.Type != AstBlockType.Main || context.MayHaveReturned || !context.IsMainFunction;
+
+            if (!context.HostCapabilities.SupportsShaderBarrierDivergence && divergent)
             {
                 context.Logger.Log("Shader has barrier on potentially divergent block, the barrier will be removed.");
 
                 return OperationResult.Invalid;
             }
 
+            // fefw: report the kept ones too, so a translation for every GPU says whether any shader has one (GitHub issue #45)
+            if (divergent)
+            {
+                context.Logger.Log("Shader has barrier on potentially divergent block, the barrier is kept.");
+            }
+
             context.ControlBarrier(
                 context.Constant(context.TypeU32(), Scope.Workgroup),
                 context.Constant(context.TypeU32(), Scope.Workgroup),
